A gas-lifted satellite well with a flowline and a riser can produce in severe slugging, in a pressure cycle or in a steady state depending on two operating decisions, the opening of the topside choke and the pressure of the lift gas at the platform, and the time-averaged production of an unstable operating point is not what a steady-state model predicts for it. This paper builds the data and the surrogate models of those two decisions and uses them to optimise the operation of the well. The well, flowline, riser and gas-lift branch are simulated with the four-equation two-fluid model of a companion study, with the flow pattern identified dynamically and the gas-lift line, annulus and check valve simulated with the same model and controlled only by the platform gas pressure. Three sampling campaigns over the two decisions, in rounds of Sobol' points every one of which starts from the final state of the base run, with an adaptive horizon that detects the established regime and captures three cycles, produced 291 settled runs in three days on a laptop over 20 to \SI{260}{\bar} of lift gas, plus 29 budget-limited runs in the corner of high opening and high gas pressure, where the liquid outflow at the riser top cycles every minute and the integrator crawls; each run is reduced to time-averaged markers (mean, minimum, maximum and the two extreme frequencies) of pressure, void fraction and phase flow rates at eight characteristic points of the network. The map of the decisions shows a stability boundary that runs from \SI{80}{\bar} of lift gas with the choke nearly closed to \SI{125}{\bar} with it open, continuous injection modulated by the cycle below it, and a production gain of \SI{34}{\percent} above it at \SI{160}{\bar} and \SI{40}{\percent} at \SI{250}{\bar}. Two surrogates of the mean liquid, gas and lift-gas rates and of their spans were trained on the markers: a neural network and a physics-informed network whose physics are the time-averaged mass balances of the well, the gas-lift branch and the valve, exact for the averages and evaluated at collocation points without simulation. With 204 training runs and 4000 epochs, the physics-informed network predicts the mean liquid rate with a test coefficient of determination of 0.96 against 0.90 for the data-only network, both predict the gas and lift-gas rates with 0.997 to 0.998, and the physics act as a regulariser: the data-only network overfits beyond a few hundred epochs, its maximum liquid rate falling from 0.79 to 0.68, while the physics-informed one, with four inequalities that tie the maxima to the means and spans, keeps that maximum at 0.82 and the liquid span at 0.76, reduces the residual of the well balance four times and halves the error of the gas rates with ten training runs; the spans of the gas rates, the signature of slugging, remain the quantity that neither network predicts reliably. The surrogate then enters an operational optimisation of the two decisions: the revenue of the mean liquid rate minus the cost of compressing the lift gas from \SI{5}{\bar} to the platform pressure, with the predicted maxima of the lift-gas and liquid rates bounded by the compressor and separator capacities. With a compressor of \SI{1.75}{\kilogram\per\second} as the capacity constraint, at the prices of the example, the compression costs one per cent of the revenue and the optimum is at about \SI{227}{\bar} with the compressor as the active constraint and the choke opening indifferent above 0.7; the objective is nearly flat along the constraint, smaller compressors move the optimum down the valve law to the edge of the stable region, and an operating curve, the optimal opening and margin for each platform pressure, shows the choke open from 110 to \SI{226}{\bar} and throttled beyond the compressor limit. The campaign is the whole cost of the method, 75 process-hours of simulation against a second of training and a fraction of a second for every optimisation, which on the simulator would take a year.
